       The Perl build system on *nix-like systems starts with the _C_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_e
       script in the root directory.

       Platform-specific pieces of the build system also live in platform-
       specific directories like _w_i_n_3_2_/, _v_m_s_/, etc.  Windows and VMS have
       their own Configure-like scripts, in their respective directories.

       The _C_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_e script (or a platform-specific similar script) is
       ultimately responsible for generating a _M_a_k_e_f_i_l_e from _M_a_k_e_f_i_l_e_._S_H.

       The build system that Perl uses is called metaconfig. This system is
       maintained separately from the Perl core, and knows about the platform-
       specific Configure-like scripts, as well as _C_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_e itself.

       The metaconfig system has its own git repository. Please see its README
       file in <https://github.com/Perl/metaconfig> for more details.

       The _C_r_o_s_s directory contains various files related to cross-compiling
       Perl. See _C_r_o_s_s_/_R_E_A_D_M_E for more details.

   _A_U_T_H_O_R_S
